D&K Creations — Baker AI Assistant: Architecture Constitution

This file is BINDING. It was extracted from an architectural audit and three
implementation phases already built and verified (`tsc --noEmit` clean, live
401-tested) by a prior architecture session. If any instruction here conflicts
with a general pattern you know from training data (especially older Vercel
AI SDK examples, tutorials, or blog posts), **this file wins**. Do not
"helpfully" revert to a more familiar but outdated pattern.

Stack: Next.js 16 (App Router) · MongoDB Atlas via the **native driver only**
(no Prisma, no Mongoose, no ORM) · Firebase Auth · Resend · Pusher ·
Cloudinary · Square · Vercel AI SDK **v7**.

---

## 1. Security Mandate — Non-Negotiable

**Every** backend API route and **every** Server Action must call
`verifyAdminAPI()` as the literal first executed statement, before any
`clientPromise`/DB call, before parsing the request body, before anything
else. On failure, throw/return an unauthorized result — never continue.

```ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const session = await verifyAdminAPI();
  if (!("user" in session)) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "Unauthorized" }, { status: 401 });
  }
  // ...rest of the handler
}
```

Server Actions are publicly invocable POST endpoints. The edge proxy
(`src/proxy.ts`) does **not** protect them automatically — it only matches
`/bakery-manufacturing-orders/*` and `/api/admin/*` by path. This is why rule
1a exists:

**1a. Every AI route MUST live under `/api/admin/ai/*`.** This is not a
naming preference — it is what makes the route inherit the edge-proxy
admin-session gate. A route placed anywhere else is unprotected at the edge
even if `verifyAdminAPI()` is present inside the handler; keep both layers.

**1b. Never trust `customerId` for identity or authorization.** Guest
checkouts have no `customerId`. It is a convenience field, not an identity
key — see §4.

**1c. Ad-hoc outbound email is forbidden until a dedicated route exists.**
Resend is currently wired to fixed React Email templates only. Any tool that
"sends a message" must draft into a `DraftMessageCard` (copy / wa.me / sms:
deep link) — never call Resend directly from a tool. Real ad-hoc email
requires a future `POST /api/admin/messages/send` with recipient validation
(must match an email already on the order) and a mandatory human preview
before send.

---

## 2. Database Rules (MongoDB)

- **Native driver only.** No ORM, no schema-validation library replacing
  Zod. Types live in `src/types/index.ts`.
- **Always project.** Every query a tool or AI route makes must exclude
  heavy fields at the driver level (not filtered after fetch) — this is what
  keeps LLM context windows small and cheap:

```ts
const ORDER_PROJECTION = {
  notesLog: 0,
  paymentToken: 0,
  referenceImages: 0,
  "items.imageUrl": 0,
  "items.imageUrls": 0,
  "items.selectedConfig": 0,
} as const;
```

- **Indexes are mandatory, not optional**, on any collection a tool queries
  repeatedly:
  - `orders`: `{ "deliveryInfo.deliveryDates.date": 1 }`, `{ status: 1 }`,
    `{ isPaid: 1 }`, `{ createdAt: -1 }`
  - `custom_orders`: `{ status: 1 }`, `{ date: 1 }`
  - Handle index-already-exists gracefully (error codes 85/86 = conflict,
    not failure); one collection failing must not abort the others.

- **Never surface a raw ObjectId to the model or the UI.** Resolve through
  `getHumanReadableLookupMaps()` (categories, flavors, shapes, diameters —
  60s TTL cache, stale-cache-over-empty-map on read failure). **Denormalized
  names already stored on the document (`item.flavor`, `tier.flavorName`)
  always take priority over the live lookup map** — they are a historical
  snapshot and are more accurate than "what the catalog is named today."

- **Strip nulls before they reach the model.** Every object handed to the
  LLM passes through a `compact()` helper that drops `undefined`, `null`,
  empty strings, and empty arrays. Empty keys invite hallucination.

- **Custom order documents are `[key: string]: any` with legacy fields.**
  Never read `custom_orders` raw — always go through
  `normalizeCustomOrder()` first, in tools and in routes alike.

- **Any free-text search input that reaches `$regex` must be escaped**
  (`escapeRegex()`) before use — unescaped input is a live ReDoS /
  broken-query vector. `_id` cannot take `$regex` directly — use
  `{ $expr: { $regexMatch: { input: { $toString: "$_id" }, regex, options: "i" } } }`.

- **Do not perform read-modify-write on whole-document arrays from a tool.**
  `PUT /api/admin/schedule-settings` rewrites the entire `dateOverrides[]`
  array and will race with a baker editing the calendar concurrently. Any
  mutation tool touching a single date must use an atomic operator
  (`arrayFilters` + `$set` on `dateOverrides.$[el]`, or `$pull`+`$push`) via a
  dedicated `PATCH` endpoint — never the bulk `PUT`.

---

## 3. Customer Identity Rule

`orders.customerId` is populated **only** for logged-in customers. Guest
checkout orders never have it. **Do not build customer identity, CRM
lookups, or LTV around `customerId`.** Identity must be resolved from
normalized phone or email:

- `orders.customerInfo.{phone,email}`
- `custom_orders.contact.{phone,email}`

Phone matching must tolerate formatting (`(403) 555-1234` vs `4035551234`) —
either match on a digits-only regex (`\D*`-joined) or, preferably, a
denormalized `phoneDigits` field written at insert time with a supporting
index. Never full-collection-scan a `$regex` phone match in a hot path.

---

## 4. Vercel AI SDK v7 — Chat / Generative UI (STRICT)

### Explicitly BANNED

- `ai/rsc` — experimental/legacy in v7.
- `streamUI` — experimental/legacy in v7.
- `generateObject` as a standalone function — **removed** in v7; structured
  output is part of `generateText`/`streamText` now.
- Rendering tool results by switching on `part.type === 'tool-invocation'`
  or `part.type === 'tool-result'` — that is v4/v5 naming and does not exist
  in v7.
- `needsApproval` on the tool definition — **deprecated**; approval moved to
  the route config (see §6).

### MANDATED pattern

Client: `useChat` from `@ai-sdk/react`, reading `message.parts`.

Server route (must be under `/api/admin/ai/chat`, must call
`verifyAdminAPI()` first):

```ts
const result = streamText({
  model,
  messages: await convertToModelMessages(messages),
  tools: bakerTools,
  stopWhen: isStepCount(6),
  toolApproval: { blockDate: "user-approval", sendReminder: "user-approval" },
});
return createUIMessageStreamResponse({
  stream: toUIMessageStream({ stream: result.stream, onError }),
});
```

Always override `onError` — the SDK default masks every failure as
"An error occurred," which is useless for debugging tool failures.

**Each tool gets its own part type, `tool-${toolName}`** (e.g.
`tool-findOrders`, `tool-getDailyBrief`) — not a generic `tool-invocation`.
Switch on this specific type, and let `part.output` narrow automatically
from the tool's registered schema (no `as` casts needed if the tool registry
is typed and exported through a `type`-only import, e.g.
`InferUITools<BakerTools>`).

**Every generative-UI component must render all four part states** — there
is no optional state here:

- `input-streaming` → skeleton, ideally showing the in-flight query
- `input-available` → skeleton (tool executing)
- `output-available` → the real card
- `output-error` → an explicit error block

**Always render an explicit empty-result state.** If a tool returns zero
rows, show that plainly — do not let the model fill the silence with an
invented answer.

Keep the LLM API key server-side only. Never `dangerouslyAllowBrowser`,
never ship a provider key to the client.

---

## 5. Vercel AI SDK v7 — Inline Form Helpers (non-chat)

For "Generate with AI" buttons on a form (product creation, quote
suggestion) — this is a **different pattern from chat, do not reuse
`useChat`**:

- Server: a dedicated (non-chat) route using `streamText` with
  `output: Output.object({ schema })`.
- Client: the `useObject` hook, which streams a `DeepPartial` of the schema
  so form fields fill in progressively — not all at once.
- This is why `generateObject` being removed matters: structured output for
  forms now flows through `streamText`, not a separate function.

Every suggested numeric value (price, especially) must be grounded by
injecting real price anchors into the prompt context (`categories.basePrice`,
`diameters.basePrice`, `gallery_images.decorationPrice`, historical
`agreedPrice` on similar converted requests) — never let the model invent a
price from nothing.

---

## 6. Human-in-the-Loop — Mutation Rule

**Every tool that mutates data must be registered with
`toolApproval: 'user-approval'`** on the chat route config (not on the tool
itself — that's the deprecated pattern). The baker must explicitly
Approve/Deny in the UI before the tool body executes. This applies to, at
minimum: blocking/unblocking dates, setting day capacity, converting a
custom request to an order, generating a payment link, rejecting a custom
request, and sending any reminder/message.

Every approved mutation must be written to the `ai_action_log` collection
(adminUid, tool name, input, output, `approvedAt`, `targetCollection`,
`targetId`, `createdAt`). Without this, an action taken a week ago (e.g. "why
is Saturday blocked?") is unrecoverable and untrustworthy. Do not ship a
mutation tool without a corresponding log write in the same code path.

---

## 7. Calculation Rule — No Math In The Model, Ever

The LLM **never** performs arithmetic that has a financial or physical
consequence. It selects inputs and calls a tool; a pure TypeScript function
does the math and the tool returns the finished number. This is not a style
preference — the model is documented to make confident, wrong arithmetic,
and a wrong gram count or wrong dollar total is a real-world loss.

Applies to (non-exhaustive — extend this list, never remove from it):

- Order/cart pricing → `calculateOrderPricing()`
- Custom order pricing → `calculateCustomOrderTotal()`
- Kitchen capacity/minutes → `computeDayCapacity()`
- Money totals on unpaid-orders aggregation → done in Mongo (`$group`), not
  summed by the model
- Relative date resolution ("next Friday," "in two weeks") →
  a dedicated `resolveDate({ expression })` tool built on `getBakeryToday()`
  - `date-fns`, evaluated in the `America/Edmonton` timezone (DST-aware).
  The model must call this tool for any relative date — it must not compute
  the date itself.
- Recipe scaling → `src/lib/recipes/scale.ts` (see below)

### Recipe scaling formula (must be implemented exactly this way)

Scale by **area**, not by diameter ratio — diameter-ratio scaling is the
common wrong answer and under-portions the cake:

```
factor = Σ(targets) [ (π · (d_target/2)²) / (π · (d_source/2)²) × layersRatio × count ]
```

- Round pans: `π·r²`. Square/rectangular pans: `side²` (or `w×h`), not
  `π·r²`.
- 6" → 8" is `(4²/3²) = 1.78×`, **not** `8/6 = 1.33×`.
- Cupcakes/units: linear scaling by `unitCount`, not area.
- Round after scaling: grams to the nearest 1, teaspoons to the nearest
  0.25.
- Any ingredient with `isScalable: false` (leavening, salt, etc.) is copied
  through **unchanged** — never scaled linearly with the rest.
- The model's job here is to pick the recipe and the target sizes and call
  `scaleRecipe(...)`; it must not "double-check" or restate the arithmetic
  itself in prose.

---

## 8. New Collections (create with these shapes, do not improvise)

```ts
// ai_conversations — chat threads
{ _id, adminUid, title, messages: [...], createdAt, updatedAt }

// ai_action_log — audit trail for every approved mutation
{ _id, adminUid, tool, input, output, approvedAt, targetCollection, targetId, createdAt }

// recipes — for the Recipe Scaler tool
{
  _id, name, slug /* unique */, isActive,
  categoryId?, flavorId?, description?,
  yield: {
    sizeLabel: string,
    diameterId?, shapeId?,
    panDiameterInches?: number,   // REQUIRED numeric field — diameters.name
                                   // is a display string, not reliably numeric
    panSideInches?: number,       // square pans
    panHeightInches?: number,
    layers?: number, servings?: number, unitCount?: number,
  },
  ingredients: [{
    id, name, quantity, unit: 'g'|'kg'|'ml'|'l'|'tsp'|'tbsp'|'pcs',
    group?, isScalable: boolean, allergenIds?: ObjectId[],
    costPerUnit?, note?,
  }],
  steps?: [{ order, instruction, durationMinutes? }],
  activeTimeMinutes?, bakeTimeMinutes?, notes?,
  createdAt, updatedAt,
}
```

Recipe indexes: `{ slug: 1 }` unique, `{ flavorId: 1 }`, `{ isActive: 1 }`.
`allergenIds` must reuse the existing `allergens` collection — this is what
lets an allergy cross-check against `custom_orders.allergies` come for free.

---

## 9. Provider / Environment

- Provider selection is lazy and env-driven: OpenAI first if
  `OPENAI_API_KEY` is set, else Anthropic if `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set.
  Model overrides: `COPILOT_OPENAI_MODEL` / `COPILOT_ANTHROPIC_MODEL`.
- If neither key is set, the route must return a clear `503` ("No AI
  provider configured") — not a silent failure.
- A **separate vision-capable model** is needed for any tool reasoning over
  `referenceImages` (Cloudinary URLs) — decor-complexity quoting, product
  generation. Do not assume the text model handles images.
- **AI SDK v7 requires Node ≥ 22.** If the project is still on Node 20.x,
  treat that as a blocking upgrade before relying on the copilot in
  production — not a warning to ignore.
- Rate limiting does not exist anywhere in this project yet. Any
  agent loop with `stopWhen: isStepCount(n > 1)` multiplies model calls —
  do not ship an agent loop to production without a throttle in front of it.

---

## 10. Baseline Text — Improve, Don't Replace

`buildQuickMessage()` (in `QuickMessageCard.tsx`) is a deterministic,
already-correct generator for quote messages (price breakdown, allergy
warning, logistics, payment link, sign-off, `wa.me:` / `sms:` links). Treat
it as the few-shot baseline and the ground truth for tone. AI drafting
tools must produce output that is a refinement of this baseline (e.g. a
"Rewrite tone" pass over its output), not a from-scratch reinvention.

---

## 11. Known Prerequisites — Do Not Build The Downstream Feature First

| If building... | This must exist first |
| --- | --- |
| Date-blocking tool (Gatekeeper) | Atomic `PATCH` on a single `dateOverride` (§2) |
| Business Analyst tool | `GET /api/admin/ai/analytics-summary` — the full `/api/admin/analytics` payload (`soldItemsLog`, `detailedOrders`, etc.) is too large for context and must not be called directly |
| Customer Insights / CRM tool | Phone/email indexes + `phoneDigits` denormalization (§3) |
| Email-sending tool | `POST /api/admin/messages/send` with recipient validation + mandatory preview (§1c) |
| Recipe Scaler tool | `recipes` collection + CRUD routes (§8) |
| Any relative-date tool input | `resolveDate()` tool (§7) — never let the model compute the date inline |

If a task requires one of the right-hand-column items and it does not yet
exist in the codebase, **build the prerequisite first** and say so — do not
approximate it inline inside the feature you were asked for.
